  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe narrow bands that brought heavy snow to the higher elevations in \nportions of Central NY have since dissipated and snowfall has become \nlighter. Light to moderate snow showers will continue this morning \nand gradually end from north to south throughout the day as a \nsurface low pressure system pulls away from the coast. Some valley \nlocations across Northeast PA have yet to transition to snow due to \nmilder conditions. With temperatures climbing into the upper 30s and \nlow 40s at these locations today, snow may mix with rain but little \nsnowfall is expected in these areas. Through the early afternoon \nhours, an additional of 1 to 2 inches of snowfall will be possible \nin the higher elevations. Elsewhere, snowfall amounts will be \nminimal.\n\nTemperatures today will max out into the 30s and 40s. Breezy winds \nwill make it feel a bit blustery as wind chills will be in the 20s \nand 30s this afternoon.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nLow-level flow will become more north to northwesterly this \nafternoon as the low pressure system drifts eastward. Temperatures \nat 850mb will drop to around -7 deg C. With this cooler air passing \nover the Great Lakes, lake effect precipitation will develop this \nafternoon and continue through Sunday night. As tonight's \ntemperatures fall below freezing, this precipitation will start out \nas snow. Diurnal temperature changes will favor rain during the day \nand snow at night through the weekend. Bands of precipitation will \noccasionally extend down into Northeast PA even though the bulk of \nit will be in portions of Central NY. Additional snowfall \naccumulations will generally be a half inch or less and with the \ntransition to rain during the day, any nighttime accumulations will \nnot last long. Narrow bands that line up over the Cortland Hills \narea could lead to accumulations around an inch or slightly more. \nHeading into Monday, CMC guidance does keep the precipitation going \nthrough the morning while most other guidance end the showers due to \ndrier air moving in. This forecast did bump up PoPs and QPF amounts \nfor the weekend using blends of Consall, CMC, and NBM guidance. Even \nwith these adjustments, PoPs remained below slight chance (< 15%) \nfor Monday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA ridge of high pressure will build into the region early next week. \nLake clouds will prevent little sunshine from peeking through on \nMonday but sunny skies return on Tuesday. Daytime temperatures will \ntrend warmer throughout the week with the potential for some 60s on \nThursday and Friday. Nightly temperatures will stay cool, often \nfalling below freezing. Despite this, most of next week will be dry \nwith surface high pressure in control. Models do show a system that \npasses just to the north late on Friday and could clip some of our \ncounties with precipitation.",
